  },
  "candidate_0020": {
    "reason": "The assertion presents a specific mechanism as a universal guarantee. While weight decay often improves generalization, it is not an absolute rule; in cases of underfitting or when the optimal solution lies on the boundary of the parameter space, penalizing large weights can degrade performance. The claim lacks necessary conditions (e.g., appropriate learning rate, task complexity) to be a valid '","counterexample": "In a linear regression task where the true relationship involves very large coefficients due to scaling issues, applying strong weight decay forces coefficients toward zero, resulting in higher training and test error compared to no regularization.",
    "sound_as_stated": false
  }
}
